WEBVTT

00:00.757 --> 00:02.557
- [Voiceover] So we have g(x) being equal

00:02.557 --> 00:04.735
to x to the fourth minus x to the fifth,

00:04.735 --> 00:07.452
and what we wanna do
without having to graph g,

00:07.452 --> 00:09.528
we want to figure out at what x values

00:09.528 --> 00:12.111
does g have a relative maximum?

00:13.451 --> 00:16.612
And just to remind us what's
going on in a relative maximum,

00:16.612 --> 00:21.560
so let me draw a hypothetical
function right over here,

00:21.560 --> 00:23.884
so a relative maximum is going to happen,

00:23.884 --> 00:25.990
so you can visually inspect this,

00:25.990 --> 00:27.998
okay that looks like a relative maximum.

00:27.998 --> 00:30.806
That's kind of a top of a
mountain or top of a hill,

00:30.806 --> 00:33.861
these all look like relative
maximum and what's in common?

00:33.861 --> 00:36.987
Well the graph, the function is going from

00:36.987 --> 00:40.282
increasing to decreasing
at each of those points.

00:40.282 --> 00:42.948
It's going from increasing to decreasing.

00:42.948 --> 00:46.855
Increasing to decreasing
at either of the points,

00:46.855 --> 00:49.114
or you could say that the first derivative

00:49.114 --> 00:52.014
is going from positive to negative.

00:52.014 --> 00:54.219
So if you look at this
interval right over here,

00:54.219 --> 00:57.246
g prime is greater than zero,

00:57.246 --> 00:59.314
and then over the next interval
when you're decreasing,

00:59.314 --> 01:01.981
g prime would be less than zero.

01:02.851 --> 01:04.281
So what we really need to think about

01:04.281 --> 01:07.281
is when does g prime, so let me see,

01:09.116 --> 01:13.761
relative, we care about
relative maximum point,

01:13.761 --> 01:15.860
and so that's essentially asking

01:15.860 --> 01:17.277
when does g prime

01:20.601 --> 01:23.018
go from positive to negative?

01:25.350 --> 01:26.608
From,

01:26.608 --> 01:27.441
from,

01:28.640 --> 01:30.131
I wrote fror.

01:30.131 --> 01:32.714
From g prime greater than zero,

01:34.110 --> 01:36.277
to g prime less than zero,

01:37.308 --> 01:40.192
and the values that we
could look at or the points

01:40.192 --> 01:42.852
are our critical points
and critical points

01:42.852 --> 01:47.019
are where g prime is either
zero or it is undefined.

01:48.525 --> 01:49.744
So let's think about it,

01:49.744 --> 01:52.389
where is g prime of x equal to zero?

01:52.389 --> 01:54.497
g prime of x is equal to zero

01:54.497 --> 01:57.747
when well let's just take g prime of x.

01:57.747 --> 02:00.137
We're gonna leverage the
power rule right here,

02:00.137 --> 02:03.941
four x to the third power,
four x to the third,

02:03.941 --> 02:06.581
minus five x to the fourth.

02:06.581 --> 02:10.498
Minus five x to the
fourth is equal to zero.

02:11.914 --> 02:14.323
Let's see, we can factor
out an x to the third.

02:14.323 --> 02:16.800
So we have x to the third times four

02:16.800 --> 02:19.792
minus five x is equal to zero.

02:19.792 --> 02:24.754
So this is going to happen
when x is equal to zero.

02:24.754 --> 02:26.377
Let me not skip steps.

02:26.377 --> 02:27.792
So this is going to happen when

02:27.792 --> 02:30.188
x to the third is equal to zero,

02:30.188 --> 02:33.944
or four minus five x is equal to zero.

02:33.944 --> 02:35.613
For x to the third equaling zero,

02:35.613 --> 02:38.122
that's only gonna happen
when x is equal to zero,

02:38.122 --> 02:39.808
and four minus five x equaling zero,

02:39.808 --> 02:41.421
we'll add five x to both sides,

02:41.421 --> 02:43.444
you get four is equal to five x,

02:43.444 --> 02:47.611
divide both sides by five,
you get 4/5 is equal to x.

02:48.811 --> 02:52.115
So here these are the two places,

02:52.115 --> 02:54.994
where our derivative is equal to zero.

02:54.994 --> 02:58.245
Now are there any places where
our derivative is undefined?

02:58.245 --> 03:00.500
Well our function right over here

03:00.500 --> 03:02.801
is just a straight up polynomial.

03:02.801 --> 03:04.764
Our derivative is another polynomial,

03:04.764 --> 03:07.543
it is defined for all real numbers.

03:07.543 --> 03:10.325
So these are our two critical points,

03:10.325 --> 03:12.636
or we could even say critical values.

03:12.636 --> 03:15.382
Now let's think of what g
prime is doing on either side

03:15.382 --> 03:17.124
of these critical values,

03:17.124 --> 03:18.733
and I'll draw a little number line here

03:18.733 --> 03:21.566
to help us visualize this, and so.

03:23.359 --> 03:25.963
So there we go, little
bit of a number line.

03:25.963 --> 03:29.122
Let's see we care about
zero and we care about 4/5.

03:29.122 --> 03:32.512
So let's say this is negative 1,

03:32.512 --> 03:34.679
this is zero, this is one,

03:36.828 --> 03:40.376
and so we have one critical point at,

03:40.376 --> 03:42.068
let me do this in magenta,

03:42.068 --> 03:46.072
we have one critical point
here at x equals zero,

03:46.072 --> 03:48.145
and then we have another critical point.

03:48.145 --> 03:50.949
I will do this at x equals 4/5.

03:50.949 --> 03:53.655
So 4/5 is right around there.

03:53.655 --> 03:57.011
So that is 4/5 and let's just think about

03:57.011 --> 04:00.428
what g prime is doing in these intervals,

04:01.435 --> 04:03.632
and these critical points
are the only places

04:03.632 --> 04:07.642
where g prime might switch
sides, switch signs.

04:07.642 --> 04:10.550
So let's first think about this,

04:10.550 --> 04:12.516
let me pick some colors
I haven't used yet.

04:12.516 --> 04:13.894
So let's think about the interval

04:13.894 --> 04:16.755
from negative infinity to zero.

04:16.755 --> 04:20.630
So this is the open interval
from negative infinity to zero,

04:20.630 --> 04:22.435
and we could just plug in a value,

04:22.435 --> 04:24.463
let's try negative one,
negative one is pretty

04:24.463 --> 04:26.605
straight forward to evaluate.

04:26.605 --> 04:29.005
So let's see you have four,
you're gonna have four

04:29.005 --> 04:31.232
times negative one to the third power.

04:31.232 --> 04:33.829
So that's gonna be four
times negative one,

04:33.829 --> 04:37.996
minus five times negative
one to the fourth power.

04:39.276 --> 04:41.347
So that's just gonna be one.

04:41.347 --> 04:45.514
So let's see this is going to
be negative four minus five.

04:46.503 --> 04:50.709
Which is negative nine,
so right over here,

04:50.709 --> 04:52.714
g prime is equal to negative nine,

04:52.714 --> 04:54.192
and so we know over this whole interval,

04:54.192 --> 04:56.341
since it's to the left
of this critical point,

04:56.341 --> 05:00.258
we know that g prime
of x is less than zero,

05:02.235 --> 05:04.753
and so our function itself is decreasing

05:04.753 --> 05:08.113
over this interval and
so we know we need to go

05:08.113 --> 05:10.156
from increasing to decreasing,

05:10.156 --> 05:12.903
so you can already say well
we can't go from increasing

05:12.903 --> 05:15.055
to decreasing at this critical point,

05:15.055 --> 05:17.688
because we're already
decreasing to the left of it,

05:17.688 --> 05:18.712
but anyway let's just think about

05:18.712 --> 05:20.634
what's happening at the other intervals.

05:20.634 --> 05:24.669
So in the interval between zero and 4/5,

05:24.669 --> 05:26.209
so that interval right over there,

05:26.209 --> 05:29.482
so it's between zero and 4/5,

05:29.482 --> 05:31.366
well let's just sample a number there.

05:31.366 --> 05:35.231
Let's say the number, I don't know, 1/2?

05:35.231 --> 05:37.493
Might be fairly straightforward.

05:37.493 --> 05:41.660
So we can evaluate g prime
of 1/2, g prime of 1/2,

05:43.462 --> 05:46.661
is equal to four times
1/2 to the third power.

05:46.661 --> 05:48.499
1/2 to the third power is 1/8.

05:48.499 --> 05:52.666
So it's 4/8, or it's just 1/2 minus five

05:53.789 --> 05:57.956
times 1/2 to the fourth so
that's 5/16 minus 5/16 and

06:00.749 --> 06:03.666
so this is equal to 8/16

06:04.802 --> 06:08.461
minus 5/16 which is equal to 3/16,

06:08.461 --> 06:11.562
but the important thing is
it's equal to a positive value.

06:11.562 --> 06:14.422
So in this blue interval right over there,

06:14.422 --> 06:16.520
and actually let me put
4/5 in a different color

06:16.520 --> 06:19.237
so we see that it's not
part of that interval.

06:19.237 --> 06:23.027
So in this light blue interval right here

06:23.027 --> 06:26.349
between zero and 4/5, g prime,

06:26.349 --> 06:29.837
g prime of x is greater than zero.

06:29.837 --> 06:31.834
So we know our function is increasing,

06:31.834 --> 06:34.134
and so let's see what's
happening to the right of this,

06:34.134 --> 06:39.087
and the easiest value to
try out would just be one.

06:39.087 --> 06:42.182
So let's try out x equals one.

06:42.182 --> 06:43.745
It's in that interval.

06:43.745 --> 06:46.096
So when x equals one, I'll just write

06:46.096 --> 06:49.679
g prime of one is equal
to four minus five.

06:50.806 --> 06:53.732
Four minus five, which
is equal to negative one.

06:53.732 --> 06:56.509
So g prime of x is less than zero,

06:56.509 --> 06:59.996
g prime of x is less than zero.

06:59.996 --> 07:04.163
So our function, so we could
say g is increasing here.

07:05.541 --> 07:08.951
It is decreasing, oh
sorry let me be careful,

07:08.951 --> 07:11.381
g is decreasing here.

07:11.381 --> 07:12.853
The function itself is decreasing

07:12.853 --> 07:14.741
cause our derivative is negative.

07:14.741 --> 07:16.879
Then our function is increasing here

07:16.879 --> 07:18.391
cause our derivative is positive,

07:18.391 --> 07:20.739
and then our function is decreasing here.

07:20.739 --> 07:24.645
So at what critical
point are we going from

07:24.645 --> 07:26.629
increasing to decreasing?

07:26.629 --> 07:29.121
Well we're doing that at x equals 4/5.

07:29.121 --> 07:33.288
So we have a relative
maximum at x equals 4/5.

07:35.211 --> 07:37.453
4/5, if they said, "Well where do we have

07:37.453 --> 07:38.977
"a relative minimum point?"

07:38.977 --> 07:41.038
Well that's going to be
happen at x equals zero.

07:41.038 --> 07:43.592
We're going from decreasing to increasing,

07:43.592 --> 07:44.701
but we've answered their question

07:44.701 --> 07:47.878
of where do we find a
relative maximum point.
